.Dd September 17, 2026
.Dt TRIBBLE-CONTROL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tribble-control
.Nd power, flash and monitor the devices on an ExSYS managed USB hub
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d Ar DEV
.Op Fl D Ar FILE
.Op Fl m Ar TYPE
.Op Fl W Ar SECONDS
.Op Fl p Ar STRING
.Op Fl -openocd Ar PATH
.Op Fl F
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl -debug Ns Oo = Ns Ar FILE Oc
.Ar command
.Op command options
.Op Ar PORT | DEVNAME ...
.Nm
.Fl -man | -help | -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The hub switches VBUS on each port independently, driven over an FT232
serial line.
tribble\-control drives that line.
For a board on it that
carries a debug probe, tribble\-control additionally speaks SWD through
openocd and reads the board's console over USB CDC.
Which probe and
which chip a board needs is the devlist's business (interface= and
target=), so the family it belongs to is not built in; the bench is
nRF52 today, and that is the default, not a requirement.
Everything
else on the hub is only an electrical load \(em see PROTECTED PORTS.
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex alpha beta
stdbuf \-oL tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf connect \-\-off gamma epsilon \e
    | tee twr.log
.Ed
.Pp
It lives on the host owning the hub, alongside devlist.conf.
It is a gem, so
.Ic gem install tribble-control
\(em from a built .gem, or
.Ic rake install
in a checkout \(em puts
.Nm
on PATH with its dependencies and needs nothing further.
.Pp
A checkout with a vendored bundle works too, and is what a host that
should not have gems installed system\-wide wants:
.Ic bundle install --path vendor
followed by
.Ic bundle binstubs tribble-control
writes bin/tribble\-control, which sets that bundle up before loading
anything.
Run that one from the directory holding the bundle.
.Sh THE HUB
.Bd -literal
the host running tribble\-control
      │
      │  FT232R ──▸ /dev/ttyUSB0        the hub's control line.  It is
      │             9600 8N1            internal to the hub: it is NOT
      ▾                                 one of the 16 switched sockets.
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   ExSYS 16\-port managed USB hub                   │
│                                                                   │
│    bank 1          bank 2          bank 3          bank 4         │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐     │
│  │  1       │    │  5       │    │  9       │    │ 13       │     │
│  │  2       │    │  6       │    │ 10       │    │ 14       │     │
│  │  3       │    │  7       │    │ 11       │    │ 15       │     │
│  │  4       │    │  8       │    │ 12       │    │ 16       │     │
│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘     │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    Sixteen independently switched sockets.  What is on each is the
    devlist's business, not this diagram's: a socket may carry a board
    the tool drives over SWD, or something it only powers and never
    talks to, or nothing at all.  Sockets in the second class are what
    PROTECTED PORTS exists for.
.Ed
.Pp
Ports are numbered 1 to 16 across four internal banks of four.
These are
the hub's own numbers, the ones the devlist and every command use; whether
the sockets are silkscreened that way is another matter, so count banks
left to right and sockets top to bottom if you are looking for one by hand.
.Pp
The FT232 control adapter enumerates at the last position of the hub's
internal tree (USB path 1\-1.2.4.4), which makes it look as though it
occupies port 16.
It does not: it is wired inside the hub and is not
switched.
Port 16 is an ordinary socket like the other fifteen.
The one
consequence is that a board must never be put on port 16: \-\-method usb
would compute the path 1\-1.2.4.4 for it and address the FT232 instead.
.Pp
A port that only supplies power tells the tool nothing about what is on
it, so if you need to reboot one device in particular, establish which
socket it is physically first.
Nothing on such a port communicates with
the host, and the devlist records a name and a port, not what is plugged
in.
.Pp
The host running tribble\-control is not powered from the hub, so no hub
port can cut power to the machine you are typing on, and \-\-force is safe
in that one narrow respect.
.Pp
Requirements: Ruby 3.1, and openocd \(em everything that reaches a
board over SWD shells out to it.
The Ruby dependencies come with the gem; openocd does not, being no
gem of anybody's.
It is expected at /usr/bin/openocd and found elsewhere with
\-\-openocd; flash and reset check for it before switching anything, and
connect \-\-reset when it reaches for it.
.Sh OPTIONS
Ordering matters, and it is the thing most likely to waste your afternoon.
Global options come before the command; a command's own options come after
the command but before its sub\-action.
So:
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf \-\-force usb off 14      global, then cmd
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf usb \-\-default=false set gamma:on
.Ed
.Pp
and NOT 'usb set \-\-default=false ...', which parses the flag as an argument
to 'set' and fails with "invalid argument".
.Pp
Global options, which go before the command:
.Bl -tag -width "-W, --warm-up=SECONDS"
.It Fl d , Fl -device Ns = Ns Ar DEV
Serial line to the hub.
Auto\-detected on Linux by looking for an FTDI 0403:6001; normally
/dev/ttyUSB0.
Pass it explicitly if you have more than one FTDI adapter attached.
.It Fl D , Fl -devlist Ns = Ns Ar FILE
The device list.
Required for any power\-down at all, and by any command taking device
names.
.Ic usb on
and
.Ic usb status
work without it.
.It Fl m , Fl -method Ns = Ns Ar TYPE
Device selection:
.Cm power , usb
or
.Cm serial .
See
.Sx DEVICE SELECTION .
Rejected if the command does not support it.
.It Fl W , Fl -warm-up Ns = Ns Ar SECONDS
Delay after powering a port on, before touching the board.
Default 5.
.It Fl F , Fl -force
Switch ports the devlist does not allow.
See
.Sx PROTECTED PORTS .
.It Fl p , Fl -password Ns = Ns Ar STRING
Supplies the hub password; it does not change it.
Default
.Sq pass .
A wrong one makes the hub answer E01 and the command abort loudly, not
silently.
.It Fl r , Fl -require Ns = Ns Ar FILE Ns Op , Ns Ar FILE ...
Ruby files to load before anything else runs, for the tallies they
register.
See
.Sx TALLIES .
.It Fl -openocd Ns = Ns Ar PATH
openocd binary.
Default /usr/bin/openocd; a name with no
.Sq /
in it is looked up in PATH, which is how to reach it on a host that
keeps it elsewhere (FreeBSD: /usr/local/bin).
Checked before any port is switched, by the commands that always need
it.
.It Fl -debug Ns Oo = Ns Ar FILE Oc
Show debug output \(em chiefly the openocd command line issued for each
board, which is the thing worth having when a flash behaves oddly.
With
.Ar FILE ,
the whole log is copied there as well as shown, appending, so a long
run can be kept without being watched.
An unwritable path is refused before any port is switched.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose , Fl -no-verbose
Run verbosely.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the tribble\-control and ExSYS library versions and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Usage, including the command list.
.El
.Pp
Per\-command: 'usb' takes \-\-default=BOOL, 'connect' takes \-\-off.
Both are
described under COMMANDS.
.Sh "HUB PROTOCOL, AND WHAT IT CANNOT TELL YOU"
The hub speaks a small ASCII protocol over the FT232 line.
Recovered from
the vendor's own cusba64 binary (which ships unstripped), the complete
vocabulary is eight commands, each terminated by CR, answering 'G...' on
success and 'E01' on error:
.Bd -literal
?Q   query: an id, the port count and the firmware   no password
GP   get port states                                  no password
SP   set port states                                  password
FP   set port states, and save as the power\-on state  password
WP   save the current states as the power\-on state    password
CP   change password                                  password
RD   restore factory defaults                         password
RH   reset the whole hub                              password
.Ed
.Pp
?Q answers a single string: an id, then the port count, then the
firmware version \(em CENTOS000516v02 on a 16\-port hub running v02.
It carries no port states; cusba64 fills its On= and Off= display from
a separate GP.
.Pp
The exsys gem implements all eight as of 1.0, ?Q as #query, and the
port count it answers decides what "every port" covers rather than
being assumed.
It holds an exclusive lock on the line for the whole of a
read\-modify\-write, so two processes cannot lose each other's changes
between the GP and the SP, and it refuses an empty port list rather
than reading it as every port.
The default password is
\&'pass', right\-padded to 8 characters.
.Pp
tribble\-control only ever issues SP, never FP or WP: port states are set for
the here and now and never written to the hub's flash, so nothing it does
survives a hub power cycle.
Note also that RD, the only recovery from a
forgotten password, drops every port \(em so changing the password with CP
is close to a one\-way door on a hub carrying anything that minds losing
power without notice.
.Pp
There is no per\-port current or voltage telemetry, and no way to bolt it
on: the protocol has no such command, cusba64 contains no such code, and
USB 2.x/3.x hubs do not report per\-port draw to the host under any
circumstances.
A device's bMaxPower descriptor is what it asks for at
enumeration, not what it consumes, and a pure power load never enumerates
at all.
Measuring consumption needs an inline USB power meter or an
instrumented bench supply.
.Pp
Two of these commands are worth fearing.
RH resets the hub and RD
restores factory defaults; either drops every port, which power\-cycles
everything on the hub, reserved ports included.
tribble\-control never
issues them, and the exsys gem only does so via #reset and
#factory_reset, both of which require an explicit confirm: true.
.Sh DEVLIST
A UCL file mapping a device name to a hub port, and optionally to the
board's debug\-probe serial number.
It is what \-D/\-\-devlist points at, and it
is the only thing that tells tribble\-control which ports it may touch.
.Bd -literal
undeclared = protect            # the default; see PROTECTED PORTS
reserved   = [ 13, 14, 15, 16 ] # ports that must not lose power

alpha   { serial = '1026360216055e5b0000...97969902', port = 1    }
beta    {                                             port = 2    }
gamma   { serial = '1026360213072dde0000...97969902', port = 4    }
retired { serial = '1026360202c4dc0f0000...97969902', port = none }
.Ed
.Pp
Indentation is not significant, but separators are: two keys on one line
need a comma between them, which is why the entries above carry one and
one\-key\-per\-line entries do not.
A CMSIS\-DAP serial is 48 hex
characters and a J\-Link one 12, and both are pasted whole \(em the ones
above are elided in the middle for width.
.Pp
Every device entry must carry a port, and 'port = none' is how one says it
is a record rather than a board on the bench.
\&'retired' above is the
case: it stopped enumerating, its port was given to another board, and the
entry stays so that its serial is not lost.
Such an entry is left out of
everything \(em never selected, never switched, never flashed \(em and naming
it says so ("device 'retired' is not on the bench").
A MISSING port is an
error, not a synonym for none: deleting the port line is exactly what
happens when a port is reassigned, and inferring "gone" from a line
somebody forgot would drop a live board silently.
Two entries claiming
one port is refused at load, none excepted.
.Pp
Every key other than 'reserved', 'undeclared', 'tally' and 'types' is
a device.
A command
given no device names operates on all of them.
Names may be used anywhere
a port number can be, in every command.
.Pp
A board with no serial= cannot be reached by 'reset' or by \-\-method
serial, both of which address a board by its serial number: the run
aborts with "Device without serial".
Fix it by running 'serial' (see
RECIPES) and pasting the value in.
.Pp
Optional keys describe a board whose probe or console differs from
the default:
.Bd -literal
epsilon { serial = '000760040233', interface = jlink, baud = 115200,
          port = 7 }
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width power_cycle
.It Cm interface
The openocd interface script, without the .cfg:
.Cm cmsis-dap
(the default, a DAPLink probe such as the MDK's) or
.Cm jlink
(a J\-Link OB, as on the DWM1001\-DEV).
Used by
.Ic flash , reset
and
.Ic connect --reset .
With
.Cm jlink ,
serial= is the J\-Link serial number as udev reports it; leading zeros
are accepted.
.It Cm target
The openocd target script, without the .cfg:
.Cm nrf52
(the default, which is what the whole bench runs) or whatever openocd
calls the chip on a board of another family.
Used wherever interface= is.
The two are independent: the probe says how to reach the chip, the
target says which chip it is.
.It Cm transport
The transport openocd selects:
.Cm swd
(the default) or whatever the chip and the probe both speak, commonly
.Cm jtag .
.Cm none
selects nothing and leaves the choice to the interface script.
Making
.Cm target
a key and leaving this one fixed would be half a fix: a chip reached
over JTAG would take the right target script and then fail on a
transport it has not got.
.It Cm work_area
How much target RAM openocd may borrow for its flash algorithms,
default 0x4000, written as openocd wants it.
.Cm none
says nothing and leaves the target script to choose.
16 KB is nothing to a large part and more than some have in total, so
it belongs to the chip rather than to this tool.
.It Cm baud
The console speed
.Ic connect
opens, default 230400 (what the MDK overlay sets).
The stock DWM1001\-DEV devicetree runs its console at 115200.
.It Cm tally
Which tally reads this board's console, overriding the file's own
.Cm tally
line.
See
.Sx TALLIES .
.It Cm power_cycle
The moments at which the board must have its port powered off and on
again: one name, or a list of them.
Known:
.Bl -tag -width after-flash
.It Cm after-flash
After a successful flash.
The DWM1001\-DEV needs it: after openocd's reset init / write / reset
run its DW1000 no longer reports transmissions (the board resolves
nothing and logs "our TX timestamps are missing") until the module is
power\-cycled; a plain SWD reset is not enough.
.El
.Pp
A name nothing acts on is not an error, so a new moment can be recorded
here before the command that honours it exists.
It lives here, and not only as the
.Ic flash --power-cycle
option, so that a flash by hand on the hub cannot forget it.
.El
.Pp
\&'reserved' lists ports that must never be powered down.
\&'undeclared' says what to do with every port the file does not
mention.
Both are described under
.Sx PROTECTED PORTS .
\&'tally' sets the bench's default tally, described under
.Sx TALLIES .
\&'types' holds settings a device can inherit, described next.
.Ss Types
A bench is usually a handful of boards of two or three kinds, and what
a kind is \(em which probe, which chip, which transport, how fast its
console runs \(em is the same on every board of that kind.
Written out per device that is the same four lines eleven times, and a
reader has to compare them all to find the one that differs.
.Pp
\&'types' says it once:
.Bd -literal
types {
  nrf52840-mdk {
    interface = cmsis-dap
    target    = nrf52
    transport = swd
    baud      = 230400
  }
  dwm1001-dev {
    interface   = jlink
    target      = nrf52
    transport   = swd
    baud        = 115200
    power_cycle = after-flash
  }
}

alpha { type = nrf52840-mdk, serial = '1026...9902', port = 1 }
delta { type = dwm1001-dev,  serial = '000760040233', port = 7 }
epsilon { type = nrf52840-mdk, baud = 9600, port = 2 }
.Ed
.Pp
A device's own key wins over its type's: a type is what a kind of board
has in common, and an entry saying otherwise is saying it about itself.
\&'epsilon' above is an nRF52840\-MDK whose console has been moved.
.Pp
Three rules, each of them refusing something at load rather than
letting it read as working:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A type nothing defines is an error.
A board that asked for the jlink type and silently got the cmsis\-dap
default is a flash through the wrong probe, reported as a success.
.It
A type may not set
.Cm port
or
.Cm serial .
Both name one particular board \(em a port is where a single board is
plugged in, a serial is one physical probe \(em so a type that set
either would be saying that every board of that kind is the same board.
.It
Types do not nest.
A type is a block of settings, not a thing that can have a type of its
own.
.El
.Pp
A misspelled 'reserverd' becomes a device named reserverd, which would
silently unprotect every reserved port.
That one is now caught: every
device entry must carry a port, so the misspelling is refused at load
with "devlist entry 'reserverd' has no port".
Validation stops there,
though \(em a serial, an interface or a baud that is wrong is wrong
silently \(em so after editing this file still run 'usb status' and read
the names and the protected column before you trust it.
.Sh PROTECTED PORTS
Some things on the hub are there for power alone: a single\-board computer,
a powered peripheral, anything the bench feeds but does not drive.
Nothing
communicates with them, so nothing notices if their VBUS disappears \(em they
simply reboot, uncleanly, mid\-write.
Two devlist keys keep that from
happening.
.Pp
\&'undeclared' decides the fate of every port the devlist does not mention:
.Bl -tag -width "undeclared = protect"
.It Li "undeclared = protect"
The default.
Only ports declared in the devlist may be powered down.
.It Li "undeclared = switch"
Any of the 16 may be powered down.
.El
.Pp
\&'reserved' names ports that are never powered down whichever mode is in
force, so it protects a port even when that port is a declared device:
.Bd -literal
reserved = [ 13, 14, 15, 16 ]
.Ed
.Pp
Together:
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control powers down a port only if the devlist allows it,
and never one the devlist reserves.
.Ed
.Pp
This is enforced at every path that can cut power, not just the obvious
one: 'usb off', 'usb toggle', 'usb set' with a port set to off, the
all\-ports\-off that begins \-\-method power, and 'connect \-\-off'.
You do not
have to reason about which commands are dangerous; they all go through the
same gate.
.Pp
So a bare 'usb off' means "every switchable port", not "all 16".
Naming a
protected port is an error, not a silent skip:
.Bd -literal
$ tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf usb off 14
tribble\-control: refusing to power down port(s) 14: not switchable,
being undeclared or reserved (use \-\-force)
.Ed
.Pp
Without a devlist there is no safe set, so power\-down is refused outright.
.Pp
\-F/\-\-force lifts both rules and restores the old "all 16 ports" behaviour.
It is the only way to deliberately reboot a power\-only device, and it is a
hard VBUS cut, not a shutdown: the device loses power mid\-write, with
whatever consequences that has for its filesystem.
Shut it down over the
network first if you care about what is on it.
It cannot strand you,
though \(em the host runs off its own supply, not off the hub.
.Pp
Powering *on* is never guarded: 'usb on' with no arguments still turns on
all 16 ports, which is the state the bench should normally sit in.
.Sh DEVICE SELECTION
Flashing one of six identical boards means telling openocd which one.
There are three ways, chosen with \-m/\-\-method:
.Bd -literal
┌─────────┬─────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────┐
│ method  │ addresses a     │ requires     │ power while the job    │
│         │ board by        │              │ runs                   │
├─────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ serial  │ its debug probe │ serial= for  │ every declared port    │
│         │ serial number   │ every board  │ on; boards in parallel │
├─────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ usb     │ its USB path,   │ Linux (not   │ every declared port    │
│         │ root.bank.slot  │ FreeBSD)     │ on; boards in sequence │
├─────────┼─────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ power   │ being the only  │ nothing      │ exactly one board at   │
│         │ board powered   │              │ a time, rest cut       │
└─────────┴─────────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────────┘
.Ed
.Pp
Which to use:
.Bl -tag -width serial
.It Cm serial
The default for
.Ic flash
and
.Ic reset .
Boards stay powered and are done in parallel, so it is much faster and
far less disruptive.
Aborts if any selected board lacks a serial=.
.It Cm power
Needs no configuration at all \(em it identifies a board by being the
only one powered \(em and is the fallback for a board whose serial= is
missing or wrong.
Slowest by far: every board costs a power cycle plus the warm\-up, so
the whole bench is sequential rounds.
It also leaves every switchable declared board POWERED OFF when it
finishes, because each port is switched off again after its turn.
A board whose port the devlist protects stays powered, and says so.
Run
.Ic usb on
afterwards to bring the bench back up.
.It Cm usb
What
.Ic connect
uses.
Addresses by USB topology, so it needs no serials, but it is
Linux\-only.
.El
.Pp
Each command accepts only the methods that make sense for it, and uses the
first as its default:
.Bd -literal
command    methods accepted          default
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-    \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-          \-\-\-\-\-\-\-
usb        (none: acts on ports)     n/a
serial     usb, power                usb
flash      serial, power             serial
reset      serial                    serial
connect    usb, serial               usb
.Ed
.Pp
\-\-method usb derives the board's USB location from the hub's own geometry,
four banks of four:
.Bd -literal
port 5 ─┬─▸ bank 2      bank is (port minus 1) div 4, plus 1
        └─▸ slot 1      slot is (port minus 1) mod 4, plus 1

hub root path .............  1\-1.2
openocd adapter usb location 1\-1.2.2.1
                             └───┘ │ │
                               │   │ └── slot
                               │   └──── bank
                               └──────── hub root
.Ed
.Pp
The location is documentation of intent rather than a selector: openocd
0.12 ignores 'adapter usb location' for CMSIS\-DAP, reaching the same
adapter whichever path it is given, including one that does not exist.
What selects is 'adapter serial', which this method passes as well
whenever the devlist has one.
A board with no serial= is therefore
addressed by nothing in particular, and with more than one adapter
powered that is a board chosen at random.
.Pp
After any power\-on, \-W/\-\-warm\-up seconds (default 5) pass before the boards
are touched, to let them enumerate.
.Sh COMMANDS
Every command takes device names or port numbers interchangeably, and
operates on all declared devices when given neither.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic usb Cm status | on | off | toggle | set Oo Ar PORT | DEVNAME Oc ...
Drive hub port power directly.
\&'set' takes PORT:STATE pairs, where
STATE is any of on/off/1/0/true/false/t/f.
With no arguments, 'on'
means all 16 ports, while 'off' and 'toggle' mean every switchable
port (see PROTECTED PORTS).
.Pp
\-\-default=BOOL sets what happens to ports 'set' does not name; it goes
before the word 'set'.
Without \-\-force it is applied only to
switchable ports, so it cannot sweep a reserved port off.
\&'status' is read\-only: it asks the hub for its port states and prints
every port with its device name, its state, and whether the devlist
lets tribble\-control switch it.
Run it before anything involving
\-\-force.
Note that 'usb on' does not wait out \-\-warm\-up; it returns as soon as
the hub acknowledges.
.Pp
Selection method: none.
Exit status: 0, or 1 on error.
.It Ic serial Oo Ar PORT | DEVNAME Oc ...
Report each board's debug\-probe serial number, read from the USB
descriptor the kernel already holds.
Prints one line
per board, "Serial for alpha: 1026...".
Use it to populate the devlist.
.Pp
Selection methods: usb (default), power.
Under
.Cm usb
it powers the boards' own
ports up and leaves the rest of the bench alone \(em asking a board its
serial is not a reason to black out the hub \(em but it walks the USB
tree, so it is Linux only.
.Cm power
is the one that works anywhere, at the price of cutting the bench; see
.Sx TRAPS .
Exit status: 1 if any
board could not be read.
.Pp
The serial comes from the operating system rather than from openocd,
which does not report it: openocd 0.12 prints neither a CMSIS\-DAP
"Serial# =" line nor a J\-Link "S/N" one, at any debug level.
Asking
the OS also means no board has to be the only one powered, which
asking openocd would have required, since it cannot choose between
adapters by itself.
.It Ic flash Ar FIRMWARE Oo Ar PORT | DEVNAME Oc ...
openocd 'flash write_image erase FIRMWARE' then 'reset run', against
the board's target= script (nrf52 by default) over its interface=
script (cmsis\-dap by default), on its transport= (swd by default).
FIRMWARE is resolved by
openocd relative to the current directory.
Every selected board is attempted even if an earlier one failed; the
exit status reflects the whole run.
A board whose devlist entry lists after\-flash under power_cycle (see
DEVLIST: the DWM1001\-DEV) has its port powered off and on again after
a successful flash; \-\-power\-cycle, before FIRMWARE, does the same for
any board.
.Pp
Selection methods: serial (default), power.
Exit status: 1 if any
board failed.
.It Ic reset Oo Ar PORT | DEVNAME Oc ...
openocd 'reset run'.
.Pp
Selection method: serial only, so it cannot address a board that has
no serial= in the devlist.
Exit status: 1 if any board failed.
.It Ic connect Oo Ar PORT | DEVNAME Oc ...
Open each board's CDC console at its baud= (default 230400) and
stream it, prefixing every line with <DEVNAME>.
Every line is also handed to the board's tally, and on exit each board
prints a SUMMARY line with whatever that tally has to say.
What the lines MEAN is not decided here \(em see
.Sx TALLIES .
.Pp
Boards are streamed concurrently, one thread each, which is what
makes a two\-way\-ranging capture meaningful; the "in sequence" in the
method table above describes the order boards are set up in, not the
monitoring.
.Pp
\-\-off powers down every declared board first, for a clean start \-\-
every declared board, not only the ones named on the command line \-\-
and then powers the selected ones back up.
.Pp
\-\-reset resets each board over SWD once its console is open.
Without
it you see only what a board says from the moment the reader attaches,
which on an already\-running board is nothing at all until the next
ranging exchange: the banner and the driver's init lines \(em including
any "Failed to initialize" \(em were printed seconds after power\-on and
are gone.
Use it whenever you care about why a board is quiet, and
note that \-\-off alone does NOT substitute: the port is powered back up
before the console is opened, so boot output still escapes.
.Pp
\-\-duration=SECONDS bounds the capture; the default is 600.
.Pp
\-\-command=CMD types CMD at each board's shell once it is up (two
seconds after a \-\-reset, half a second otherwise) and is the way to
reach anything the firmware gates behind a run\-time setting.
The
case it exists for: redskin compiles spank's log level to warning,
but the ranging results are logged at info, so a capture counts zero
exchanges on a bench that is in fact working perfectly \-\-
.Bd -literal
\-\-command='spank syslog info'      # the whole info stream
\-\-command='spank report all'       # just the distances, cheaper
.Ed
.Pp
turns them on without reflashing.
Prefer the second: the report path
is written straight out by the port instead of going through the
syslog, so it is not silenced by the compiled\-in level and costs the
firmware less in a loop that is timing sensitive.
It is sent once,
to every selected board, on a separate write handle; the command is
not echoed by tribble\-control, but the board's own echo and reply
appear in the capture like any other output.
Give it once \-\-
repeating the option keeps only the last.
ALWAYS run it under 'stdbuf \-oL' if you are piping the output
anywhere.
Without that, the pipe buffers in 4 KB blocks and 'tee'
shows nothing for minutes.
.Pp
\-\-interactive is \-\-command without the limit of one command decided
in advance: standard input is forwarded to the board, a line at a
time, and the console stays open until end of input instead of for
a \-\-duration.
One device only \(em there is one standard input, and
two boards sharing it could not be told apart or addressed
separately.
What the board replies appears in the stream like
anything else, prefixed with its name, so what was typed and what
came back read in order.
It is meant to be run through a terminal \(em 'bench connect' opens it
over ssh \-t, which is what supplies the editing and the echo of the
line being typed; a pipe works too, and sends what it is given.
.Pp
Selection methods: usb (default), serial.
Exit status:
0, or 1 on error; nothing about what the boards did.
.El
.Sh TALLIES
.Nm
opens a board's console, prefixes each line with the board's name and
prints it.
What a line MEANS it does not know, and deliberately does not: the
strings worth counting belong to whatever firmware is on the bench this
month, they change when that firmware changes, and none of them are
facts about a USB hub.
.Pp
A tally is where that knowledge goes.
.Ic connect
hands it every line and asks it, once the capture ends, for the text of
the board's SUMMARY line.
Two come built in:
.Bl -tag -width lines
.It Cm lines
How many lines the board printed.
The default, and all a tool that knows nothing about the firmware can
honestly say.
.It Cm none
Nothing: the lines are printed, and no SUMMARY follows them.
.El
.Pp
Any other is a block, registered by a Ruby file named with
.Fl r :
.Bd -literal
# twr-tally.rb
class TwrTally
    def initialize(device) = @ok = 0
    def <<(line)
        @ok += 1 if line =~ /whatever this firmware calls success/
        self
    end
    def summary = "ok=#{@ok}"
end

TribbleControl::Tally.register(:twr) {|device| TwrTally.new(device) }
.Ed
.Pp
The object the block returns needs two methods:
.Ic <<
takes a line and returns anything, and
.Ic summary
returns the SUMMARY text, or nil for no SUMMARY line at all.
The block is called once per board per run, so a tally may keep
whatever state it likes without sharing it with another board's.
.Pp
Which tally a board uses is a devlist key.
At the top of the file it sets the bench's default, and inside a device
entry it overrides that for one board:
.Bd -literal
tally = twr                     # every board on this bench

D4 { port = 7, tally = lines }  # except this one
.Ed
.Pp
A name nothing has registered is an error, not a silent fall back to
counting lines: a devlist asking for twr on a run that forgot
.Fl r
would otherwise capture the whole bench and report line counts, which
reads exactly like a firmware that has gone quiet.
.Sh RECIPES
Read the serials of every board, to fill in the devlist.
This cycles the
bench and leaves it powered off:
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf serial
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf usb on
.Ed
.Pp
Add a board on a new port.
\&'serial 7' will not work: an undeclared port
has no name, and the tool refuses to power ports the devlist never
mentions.
Declare a stub first, then read the serial and fill it in:
.Bd -literal
echo "X1 { port = 11 }" >> devlist.conf
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf serial X1
.Ed
.Pp
Pick a port nothing else declares.
Two entries on one port is refused at
load time, for every command including 'usb status', so a collision here
leaves the devlist unusable until it is edited back.
.Pp
Flash the whole bench, then just two boards:
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex alpha beta
.Ed
.Pp
Flash in parallel, leaving the bench powered.
Needs a serial for every
board named, so fix the gaps first (see DEVLIST):
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf \-\-method serial flash zephyr.hex
.Ed
.Pp
Run a ranging capture to a log, for ten minutes:
.Bd -literal
stdbuf \-oL tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf connect \-\-off gamma epsilon \e
    | tee twr.log
.Ed
.Pp
Find out why a board is silent \(em capture its boot, including the driver
init lines, for one minute:
.Bd -literal
stdbuf \-oL tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf connect \-\-off \-\-reset \e
    \-\-duration=60 gamma beta | tee boot.log
.Ed
.Pp
Capture ranging results from a stock redskin build.
Without the command
the log level hides them and the SUMMARY reads ok=0 / tx\-rate=NaN:
.Bd -literal
stdbuf \-oL tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf connect \-\-off \-\-reset \e
    \-\-command='spank syslog info' \-\-duration=60 gamma beta | tee twr.log
.Ed
.Pp
Power\-cycle one board without disturbing the others:
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf usb off gamma && sleep 2 && \e
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf usb on  gamma
.Ed
.Pp
Deliberately reboot a device on a reserved port (the only case for
\-\-force).
This is a hard power cut; shut the device down over the
network first:
.Bd -literal
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf \-\-force usb off 14 && sleep 2 && \e
tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf usb on 14
.Ed
.Pp
Flash from a script, failing the build if any board failed:
.Bd -literal
#!/bin/sh
cd /root || exit 1
exec tribble\-control \-D devlist.conf flash zephyr.hex
.Ed
.Sh TRAPS
Exit status.
Any command exits 1 if it aborts with an error \(em a refused
power\-down, an unknown device name, a hub that answers E01.
On top of
that, flash, reset and serial exit 1 when they ran to completion but some
board failed; usb and connect report nothing about the work itself, so a
0 from 'connect' says only that it started.
Beware that piping into
\&'tee' hands you tee's status, not tribble\-control's \(em which is exactly
what the capture recipe does, so do not use that shape in a script
without a pipefail or a temporary file.
.Pp
\-\-method power leaves every switchable declared board powered off when
it finishes.
Nothing defaults to it any more: flash defaults to serial, reset is
serial only, and serial and connect default to usb.
You get it by asking
for it with \-m power, and then you want 'usb on' afterwards to bring the
bench back.
.Pp
connect stops after 600 seconds unless \-\-duration says otherwise; Ctrl\-C
is the other way out of a shorter run.
Under \-\-interactive there is no
timer at all: it ends on end of input (Ctrl\-D) or on Ctrl\-C.
.Pp
A SUMMARY counting nothing is not proof that a board said nothing.
A tally counts the strings one firmware prints, so the wrong tally on a
board, or the right one on a firmware whose log level hides the lines,
both report a healthy board as an idle one.
Check which tally the board is using
.Pq Sx TALLIES
and that the firmware is logging at all before concluding the radio is
dead; \-\-command is how a log level is raised at run time.
.Pp
\-\-debug is what makes a run verbose; the default is quiet.
What it exists to reveal is the openocd command line issued for each
board.
\-\-debug=FILE also writes the log where it says.
If you have a script that greps a run for its openocd invocations, it
now has to ask for them.
.Pp
FreeBSD is half usable.
Hub auto\-detection, exsys_ctrl and
probe_consoles all work there.
Note that openocd is not at
/usr/bin/openocd there: pass \-\-openocd=/usr/local/bin/openocd, or just
\-\-openocd=openocd and let PATH answer.
.Pp
What remains Linux\-only is port_to_usb, usb_to_tty and usb_to_serial,
which read /sys/bus/usb.
That rules out \-\-method usb there, and each of the three says which
piece is missing rather than dying on NoMethodError.
.Pp
.Ic connect
works anyway, with
.Fl m Cm serial :
the console is found by the serial of the probe in front of the board,
which both a DAPLink and a J\-Link OB report, and which the devlist
already carries in order to address that board for flashing.
No USB tree is walked, so nothing in that path is Linux\-only.
.Pp
.Ic serial
works there too, with
.Fl m Cm power .
It has to identify a board it has no serial for yet, which is what
.Cm power
is for: it cuts every switchable port, brings up one board at a time,
and the probe then enumerated is the only one there is.
No topology, no descriptor path, nothing Linux has that FreeBSD has
not.
It costs a cycle of the whole bench and leaves it powered off \(em run
.Ic usb on
afterwards \(em which is why
.Cm usb
remains the default on a host that can use it.
.Pp
A probe on a port the devlist protects stays powered through all of
that, so it is enumerated alongside the board being asked.
.Nm
says so and stops rather than attributing one board's serial to
another.
.Pp
\-\-method usb trusts the 4\-by\-4 geometry.
A board on port 16 would resolve
to 1\-1.2.4.4, which is where the FT232 control adapter already sits, so
port 16 is not usable for a board addressed that way.
.Pp
The hub password defaults to 'pass' (\-p/\-\-password to change it).
It
guards writes to the hub, not reads.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/bin/openocd
.It Pa devlist.conf
The device list, named with
.Fl D .
It has no default and no fixed location: it describes a bench rather
than the tool, so it lives with whatever owns the bench.
See
.Sx DEVLIST .
.It Pa /usr/bin/openocd
The openocd binary, overridable with
.Fl -openocd .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openocd 1
